  "#devlinks": "The third payload the pools collection captures, and the only one whose ROOT keys are data: an alias path per device symlink, mapping to the kernel name it dereferences to. Scoped to that payload, because the same two patterns unscoped would say zpool's schema roots ('pools', 'output_version') are data too, and the scrubber would refuse a document it understands perfectly. The key is classified identity for its TAIL \u2014 a by-partuuid link ends in the partition UUID that correlates this disk across captures \u2014 and takes the free-text pass so the tree it sits in survives: /dev/disk/by-partuuid is the kernel's own vocabulary, and which tree a vdev resolves through is the fact the payload exists to teach. The value is a kernel block name, positional on every machine of that shape, so it is 'nothing' for the same reason phys_path is.",
  "#group-vdevs": "zpool status -j carries spares, log devices and cache devices as TOP-LEVEL keys on the pool object, beside 'vdevs' rather than inside it. The manifest knew only the nested tree, so the scrubber refused to classify a payload holding any of them \u2014 which is why no capture ever staged one, and why the collection's blindness to group vdevs went unseen for as long as it did. The members are disk leaves and carry exactly what a nested leaf carries, so the classifications mirror the nested ones rather than being restated with a different opinion.",
